    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.animate\n\n### Description\n\nWith <C-d> and <C-u> rebound to <C-d>zz and <C-u>zz scrolling does not function properly.\r\n\r\nDepending on the version of neovim this problem has two variants:\r\n\r\n## 0.8.3\r\nWhen I press <C-d> in the center of a file, everything works great. But if you are at the very top/bottom of a file the first `<C-d>` will not center the screen. The next press will attempt to scroll, fail, jump back to the current position, but this time with the screen centered. Continuing down will now succeed for the rest of the file.\r\n\r\n## 0.9.0\r\n`<C-d>` and `<C-u>` fail, always returning to the original cursor position.\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.8.3 and nightly 0.9.0-dev-857+g2c5906b55\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Rebind <C-d> and <C-u> to center the buffer with zz\r\n````\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"<C-d>\", \"<C-d>zz\")\r\nvim.keymap.set(\"n\", \"<C-u>\", \"<C-u>zz\")\r\n````\r\n2. Hit C-d at the top of a long file.\r\n3. Hit C-u at the bottom of a long file.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe buffer should scroll down and center the screen when <C-d> is pressed from the top of a file.\r\nAlternatively, it should scroll up and center the screen when <C-u> is pressed from the bottom of a file.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\n## 0.8.3\r\nThe buffer scrolls down but does not center the screen. The next time <C-d> is pressed the screen starts to scroll down, then jerks back up and ends up with the cursor back at the same location, but the buffer is now centered. After this point, scrolls succeed as normal.\r\n\r\nhttps://user-images.githubusercontent.com/454563/218840867-888d99e6-e8c8-40dc-99d5-2b01a297eb18.mp4\r\n\r\n## 0.9.0\r\nThe screen scrolls but snaps back and the cursor returns to the original position. If you rapidly press <C-d> and get the command in before the buffer scrolls down to 1, you successfully scroll *a bit*.\r\n\r\nhttps://user-images.githubusercontent.com/454563/218840823-580471b1-e448-4a7c-85a0-b1c8b1b179b4.mp4",
